Packaging source for `scripts/build-deb.sh`. Notes that don't belong in
`control.in` itself (Debian control files don't support `#` comments —
caught by a real `dpkg-deb` parse failure while building this).

- `Depends:` package names in `control.in` were verified against the
  reference VM (Ubuntu 24.04 noble) via `dpkg -l` — several carry the
  noble 64-bit-time-transition "t64" suffix (e.g. `libgtk-3-0t64`).
  Hand-written, not derived via `dpkg-shlibdeps`/`dh_shlibdeps` — this
  package is built directly with `dpkg-deb`, not full debhelper. A real
  public release should verify this list with proper shlibdeps tooling
  rather than trusting this by-hand list indefinitely.
  Release review (2026-11-17): the list originally covered only the C
  libraries and missed every GObject-Introspection typelib package the
  GJS code imports (`gir1.2-webkit2-4.1`, `gir1.2-gtk-3.0`, ...). The
  bug was hidden because every test machine also had `gnome-sushi`,
  which depends on them. `tests/test-deb-depends.sh` now derives the
  imported GI namespaces from `src/` and fails if any owning package is
  missing from `Depends:` (LibreOffice's optional `LOKDocView` is in
  `Suggests:`).

- **Two-pass build, not one — a real `dpkg -i` collision caught this,
  not something assumed up front.** A first attempt built everything
  with a single `meson setup --prefix=/usr` pass, matching how a normal
  system package would be built. `dpkg -i` refused it outright:
  `trying to overwrite '/usr/bin/sushi', which is also in package
  gnome-sushi 46.0-1`. Diffing our package's file list against
  `dpkg -L gnome-sushi` (`comm -12`, see `build-deb.sh`'s own comments)
  found the full list: `/usr/bin/sushi`, the previewer binary itself
  (`/usr/libexec/org.gnome.NautilusPreviewer`), its private libs
  (`libsushi-1.0.so`, its typelib, its `.gir`), both compiled
  gresources, the gtksourceview style, and the appdata.xml — all of
  these live at the *exact same paths* the real system `gnome-sushi`
  package uses, by design (that's what lets the previewer act as a
  drop-in D-Bus-activated replacement) — but that design assumption
  only worked so far because every previous install in this project was
  per-user (`$HOME/.local`, a different prefix entirely from the
  system's `/usr`), never a true system-wide `/usr` install where the
  paths become byte-identical to the real package's own files.

  Fix: the previewer (binary + everything private to it) now builds in
  its own pass with `--prefix=/opt/makimiru/previewer` — a private,
  package-owned location that shares no paths with `gnome-sushi` at
  all, so no `Conflicts:`/`Replaces:` is needed (which would have meant
  effectively replacing the system package, violating Part I's "don't
  modify/replace system Sushi" goal). This tree needs no per-user copy
  either — it's read-only shared code, not user data. A separate pass
  with `--prefix=/usr` builds the thumbnailer and the shared `web/`
  assets (which genuinely must be under `/usr` specifically — SPEC-010's
  bwrap-sandbox finding) plus the MIME override; none of those collide
  with anything, their names are unique to this fork.

- **Only the D-Bus service file needs per-user handling — not the
  `.thumbnailer` registration too.** An earlier version of this design
  treated both symmetrically "for consistency," but the `.thumbnailer`
  file's name (`makimiru-model.thumbnailer`) is unique to this fork
  and was never actually forced off `/usr` by anything — only the D-Bus
  service file collides (upstream Sushi ships one with the identical
  filename). Simplified once that became clear: the thumbnailer and its
  registration are fully system-wide, active immediately after
  `apt install`, no login-wait needed. Only the previewer's spacebar
  activation needs the next-login step.

- Activation model (SPEC-011, user-approved): `makimiru-activate.service`
  (a systemd --user unit, auto-enabled via a
  `graphical-session.target.wants` symlink — same convention
  `update-notifier` uses on this reference machine) runs
  `makimiru-activate-user` on every graphical login to reconcile that
  one D-Bus service file against a template staged at
  `/usr/share/makimiru/activation-templates/`.

- `makimiru-activate-user` is a *reconciliation*, not a one-shot
  install action: it activates when the template exists (package
  installed) and cleans up when it doesn't (package removed) — this is
  what makes removal correct even for a user who wasn't logged in when
  `apt remove` ran (they get cleaned up on their next login instead).

- **Second, independent sandbox finding — no display server access at
  all.** After fixing the `/usr`-path collision above, the first real
  `apt install` + Nautilus grid-view test still showed no thumbnails.
  `strace -f` on the real Nautilus-triggered invocation traced through
  to the actual sandboxed child process and found `Gtk-WARNING: cannot
  open display: :0` — the bwrap sandbox provides no X11/Wayland socket
  at all (confirmed from its own invocation args: no `.X11-unix` bind,
  `/tmp` replaced with an isolated dir holding only the one input
  file). This is unrelated to the `/usr`-path constraint SPEC-010 found
  — a second, independent constraint on top of it, only visible once
  the first one was fixed and the binary could actually be reached.
  Fix (`src/makimiru-thumbnailer.in`'s `_startPrivateXServer()`):
  spawn a private, throwaway Xvfb inside the same sandboxed process
  tree before `Gtk.init()` — fully self-contained, no host display
  server needed in any context. Always spawned unconditionally, not
  conditional on an already-set `DISPLAY`, since a set-but-dead
  `DISPLAY` (what the sandbox actually inherits) is indistinguishable
  from a working one without attempting the same connection `Gtk.init()`
  itself would make. New runtime dependency: `xvfb`.

- `debian/prerm` additionally removes the same known file directly, for
  any user who *is* currently logged in at removal time — see its own
  header comment for why this is a materially safer operation than the
  "root writes new content into ~/.local" case the activation-model
  decision was actually about (this only ever deletes a file the
  package's own script created).
